[Music]  if you listen to a lot of the debates  about what's happening in Britain at the  moment you'd think we were living in  some kind of dystopia you know you hear  about the social media and the damage  it's doing to people you hear about  issues like wealth inequality or you  hear about brexit and the potential  Armageddon scenarios you know the plague  of locusts that could emerge and you  could forgiven for thinking that things  are really terrible in Britain and we're  in some kind of state of decline but I  very much disagree with that thesis  about our country in fact I think we're  on the threshold of something that is  very positive and very optimistic and  what I want to talk about today is how  we get there what are the things we need  to do from where we are now to improve  Britain and make our country a brilliant  place to live in and if you look at what  we've achieved with the economy now  after nine difficult years we're now  paying down the debt there's a  proportion of GDP for the first time  we've got Headroom in terms of the  budget brexit is of course a tricky  issue to navigate but the fact is once  we leave the European Union we will have  extra freedom for manoeuvre and I think  one of the most exciting things that's  happening now in Britain is the new  generation that's coming through and I  think this is a different generation  from ones we've seen before I describe  them as heir B and being goober riding  deliverer eating freedom fighters and I  see some people in the audience shaking  their heads but we see opinion polling  shows that younger people are more like  to set up businesses so there's been an  85 percent increase in 18 to 24  year-olds  setting up businesses in the last four  years they're more likely to have a side  hustle alongside the normal work they're  doing whether it's running a YouTube  channel whether it's renting out a  bedroom on  Airbnb whether it's running working in  the economy in different ways and I  think it is because that generation has  grown up with more freedom than previous  generations more information at their  fingertips and more capability and I  don't think it's surprising that that  generation believe in lower taxes then  older people generally do they're also  more interested in things like public  service reform so I think that shows  that there are people out there who want  more opportunities in British society  and I think that over the next few years  we've got the opportunity to get that  energy use their energy and help Britain  become a more successful economy to get  rates of economic growth up but also to  raise the opportunity of every  individual in this country to help them  live their best life so how do we do  that  well first of all the free enterprise  economy is absolutely key to that that  is the thing that gives people the  ability to be able to take control of  their own destiny you don't have freedom  over being able to live your own life if  you can't control your own money and  that's why I think it's so important  that we keep taxes low on people  particularly on those businesses that  are just starting out where people want  to take a risk they want to aspire they  want to do things differently I also  think that we need to make sure the  government is doing less better so I've  been doing a series of spending review  roundtables around the country talking  to people about where they want public  money spent and what people say is they  want money spent on core public services  like the police or education or health  making sure our roads work and I think  that is where we should be focusing our  efforts and too often I mean we have  seen a bit of a creeper creeper plan of  a government where we now have three  hundred and five different quangos and  regulators which cost 2.5 billion  and we've seen more and more regulations  placed on people's everyday lives and  businesses so is it really the business  of government to be saying to people how  big their pizza should be or what the  minimum price of a glass of wine should  be I would rather the government is  focusing on those core basics they're  going to help people get on in life and  if the ultimate devolution of power is  to the individual which I believe I  believe firmly that we need to empower  people and as people become more capable  more informed they're better placed to  take decisions than the government are  but as that happens I also think we need  to devolve more power to local  authorities Britain is than one of the  most centralized countries in the  Western world and of course part of that  decentralization is brexit that will  enable us to make more decisions here in  Britain but I don't think people will be  satisfied by voting for brexit if they  don't see more of those powers going to  their local areas rather than those  decisions being made in Whitehall one of  the things I think we should be looking  at is can we devolve more taxes there's  a very good paper out recently by the  Institute of Fiscal Studies talking  about devolving 3 pence of basic rate  income tax to be spent at a local level  and I think that would be a good thing  for various reasons first of all it  would mean that those low called bits of  local government have an incentive to  help the economy grow attract people to  the area  help people earn more money it also  provides a sustainable form of finance  for local government rather than what we  have at the moment which is very much  based on a central government grant  system and it would mean that decisions  over issues like transport could be made  by local areas about what they see as  the priority and now I am concerned  about the fact that at the moment cities  like Leeds cities like Birmingham don't  have the type of transport that they  deserve  Leeds is the biggest city in you  without it saying mass transit network  and that has been a failure of  successive governments to build that  type of infrastructure but I think the  really the way we're really going to  shift the dial on that is by actually  allowing leads and other cities and  other counties across the country to  keep more of the revenue raised there so  they can spend it on improving local  infrastructure so I think that's  something we need to do as a country we  need to make sure that more money is  both raised and spent locally to be able  to improve the local situation and I  think in doing that we need to recognize  that this concept of the postcode  lottery which often dominates British  politics we've got to be careful of  talking about that all the time because  I want different parts of Britain  different parts of England to be able to  do things differently and to be able to  have different priorities it's one of  the reasons we put in place the police  and crime commissioners say that local  priorities could be reflected and I  think we need to allow that more in our  public services so we shouldn't just  have diversity of individuals making  their own decisions about their own  lives we should also allow more  diversity in terms of the way towns  provide services in terms of the way  cities provide services in terms of the  way those decisions are made but having  said that I think there is an absolute  need to make sure that everybody in this  country has the universal basic  infrastructure to succeed now I'm not a  fan of universal basic income I think it  is a proposal that would end up paying  large amounts of money to people who are  already very well-off and the evidence  in countries like Finland is that it  hasn't worked but I do think we have an  issue of unfairness in our society which  is that some parts the country will see  fiber-to-the-home  or better broadband delivered 10 years  later than other people I don't think  that's right and I don't think it's fair  and if we are to have a successful free  enterprise economy we need to make sure  that everybody can't participate in it  we need to make sure that every child is  able to get up to a basic level of  education to have English and maths we  need to make sure that every household  is able to access the Internet and has  that fiber to their premises we need to  focus when we're thinking about  transport infrastructure on people's  journeys to work because it isn't fair  that it costs so much more to get to  work in some parts of the country than  others and in some parts of the country  this simply isn't the transport  available so I think the concept of  universal basic infrastructure is really  important otherwise people feel that  they are cut off from the opportunities  that a successful free enterprise  economy provides so these are some of  the areas that I think we should focus  on but finally we also need to recognize  the world has changed since know since  the paternalistic companies of the 1950s  I remember when I first started at work  I I work for Shell and they still had a  company dentist they they had a company  bar you know everything was provided by  the company and that's what it used to  be like for motivates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  Institute of Arts and ideas her III TV  you

